<item>
            <title>[LU-20921] sptlrpc: per-net flavor rules do not follow the net of the RPCs with Multi-Rail</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20921</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Problem&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Problem&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A per-net flavor rule, such as &lt;tt&gt;testfs.srpc.flavor.tcp1=skn&lt;/tt&gt;, matches on the net of a NID. Each side uses the primary NID of the other side:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The client: &lt;tt&gt;sptlrpc_import_sec_adapt()&lt;/tt&gt; gives &lt;tt&gt;imp_connection-&amp;gt;c_peer.nid&lt;/tt&gt; to &lt;tt&gt;sptlrpc_conf_choose_flavor()&lt;/tt&gt;. This step runs at each connect and reconnect.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The server: &lt;tt&gt;tgt_connect_check_sptlrpc()&lt;/tt&gt; gives &lt;tt&gt;req-&amp;gt;rq_peer.nid&lt;/tt&gt;, the primary NID of the client, to &lt;tt&gt;sptlrpc_target_choose_flavor()&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;With Multi-Rail and LNet Dynamic Peer Discovery, a primary NID can be on any net of the node. The primary NID can also change. One connection can send on many nets. Thus a per-net rule does not describe the net that the RPCs use. The two sides can also select different flavors. The server then refuses the RPCs with &quot;unauthorized rpc flavor&quot;. A client can also use a weaker flavor than the rule for the net in use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;A server has NIDs on &lt;tt&gt;tcp0&lt;/tt&gt; and &lt;tt&gt;o2ib0&lt;/tt&gt;. Its primary NID is on &lt;tt&gt;o2ib0&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The rules are &lt;tt&gt;srpc.flavor.tcp0=krb5p&lt;/tt&gt; and &lt;tt&gt;srpc.flavor.default=null&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;A client that sends on &lt;tt&gt;tcp0&lt;/tt&gt; selects &lt;tt&gt;null&lt;/tt&gt;, because the primary NID of the server is on &lt;tt&gt;o2ib0&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20384&quot; title=&quot;ptlrpc: sptlrpc per-network flavor rules never match (only default applies)&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-20384&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-20384&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; made per-net rules match again. Before that fix, only the default rule applied. &lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20403&quot; title=&quot;Refresh import conn on primary migration&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-20403&quot;&gt;LU-20403&lt;/a&gt; removes the lock of the primary NID. After &lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20403&quot; title=&quot;Refresh import conn on primary migration&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-20403&quot;&gt;LU-20403&lt;/a&gt;, the client side follows the primary NID that discovery reports, as the server side already did.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This analysis is source-only. No test shows the failure yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Futurework&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Future work&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Select the flavor from the net that the RPCs use, on both sides. Questions to answer:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Can the client select a flavor per LNet message, from the net of the local NI and the peer NI? A GSS context is per import, so a per-net flavor can need a context for each net.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Can the server check the flavor against the net of each received message, not against the primary NID of the client?&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Should a connection with a per-net rule send only on that net, as the &apos;network&apos; mount option does?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Until this work lands, the manual must describe the current behavior. A separate LUDOC ticket covers that note.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
